## 20181107

0.6.2: Don't install homebrew or tor needlessly if issuing Homebrew is already installed! tor is already installed!.

1. + homebrew_installed?()
2. ~ install_homebrew() to make use of homebrew_installed?().
3. ~ install_tor() to make use of tor_installed?(), though this is a bit redundant, since setup() should control whether install_tor() is run anyway.
4. ~ setup(), so that it doesn't needlessly install brew, because tor is already installed.


## 20181107

0.6.1: Fixed missing libraries and updated Version.rb.

1. + lib/String/grep.rb, which had been left out after attempting to gather together all the requisite libraries for each version of startor prior to putting into a git repo and publishing yesterday.  I am needing to just create a repo from dot, have tests in place, and/or a sandbox to ensure that libraries I already have installed don't mislead me into thinking that I have all the dependencies intalled.
2. + lib/String/each.rb, which is a dependency of String#grep's.
3. + lib/String/each_char.rb, which is a dependency of String#each's.
4. + lib/String/each_line.rb, which is a dependency of String#each's.
5. Updated lib/Version.rb, so that: /require 'String/capture'/require 'Thoran/String/Capture/capture'/, which I should have done as of startor 0.3.0 when gathering the libraries, since that post-dates the change to Version.rb.
6. - lib/String/capture.rb
7. ~ .gitignore: + *.gem, so that gem builds don't go in the repo without me having to remove them manually when I do `git add .`.


## 20181106

0.6.0: Gemification and further tidying...

1. + startor.gemspec
2. Added to the section of comments entitled "Installation", which includes making use of the startor gem.


## 20181101

0.5.2: Check for tor rather than for brew when installing it.

1. ~ install_tor(), so that it actually checks for the presence of tor and not copy-pasta brew!
2. Removed the section of comments entitled "Suggested method for installation of prerequisites", since that is captured within setup() and friends.
3. Added to the section of comments entitled "How to use", to include needing to enter one's password, use of `startor setup`, and <ctrl> and <c> for quitting tor.


## 20181029

0.5.1: Ask whether tor is installed in one place.

1. + tor_installed?()
2. ~ check_for_tor_program() to make use of tor_installed?().
3. ~ main() to make use of tor_installed?().


## 20181028

0.5.0: An attempt to make this easy to use.

1. ~ main(), so that it has an additional subcommand: "setup" and so that it doesn't run the down method if tor is found to not be installed.
2. + install_homebrew()
3. + install_tor()
4. + setup()
5. ~ check_for_tor_program(), so that it outputs additional instructions on how to install tor via the setup subcommand.


## 20181028

0.4.0: Decided that I wanted to have the functionality of both 0.2.0 which startor remains running and 0.3.0 which passes control from startor to tor.

1. ~ main(), so that it will call either explicit_up() or implicit_up().
2. + explicit_up(), which is called when the subcommand 'up' is used.
3. + implicit_up(), which is called when no subcommand is supplied.
4. ~ up(), so that it does all the preparatory work it previously did, except start tor itself.
5. /start_tor()/system_tor()/, which keeps startor running.
6. + exec_tor(), which passess control from startor to tor.
7. Added some instructions on how to use.


## 20181028

0.3.1: Fold the colour methods into puts_with_colour().

1. + puts_with_colour().
2. /green()/puts_green()/ and call puts_with_colour().
3. /orange()/puts_orange()/ and call puts_with_colour().
4. Added some installation instructions.


## 20181028

0.3.0: Bring tor down through down(), and colour the output.

1. /require 'IfConfig'/require 'OSX/IfConfig'/
2. ~ startor() so that it uses system again, since an interrupt won't be trapped and the proxy won't be brought down.
3. ~ main() so that it calls down() instead of going directly to disable_proxy(), since I may want to do more when bringing tor down.
4. + green() so that this is easier to read.
5. + orange() so that this is easier to read.


